        "title": "Conservation Zoology and Defaunation",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Conservation zoology applies the full toolkit of zoological science — systematics, ecology, behavioral ecology, physiology, genetics — to the problem of biodiversity loss. The field has undergone a conceptual shift since the 1990s: from species-centered rescue operations to ecosystem-level thinking, from static preservation to dynamic process restoration. The central empirical reality is defaunation: the systematic loss of animal biomass, abundance, and diversity from ecosystems at all scales, with cascading functional consequences that extend far beyond the species lost."
    }
